//? Title: Compact BigGAN: Hierarchy, Projection and Captured Statistics
//? Subtitle: An editable 8x 8 class-conditional experiment with explicit spectral and BN writes
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A small class-conditional residual GAN fits 1000 balanced MNIST digits resized to 8x 8.
Twelve Gaussian coordinates split into three chunks: one seeds a 4x 4 feature map and
two join a shared class embedding to condition separate batch-normalization layers.
A projection discriminator scores each image/class pair. Every /W kernel uses cached
spectral normalization; G also receives a modified raw-kernel orthogonal penalty.
Two Adam states perform 96 writes each across three fixed epochs. G's moving BN moments
are captured in the same forward as its gradient, then committed after its Adam write.
The complete native program preserves every original artifact, including rejection
proposal audits, component derivatives and weak conditional samples. Separate inference
and batch-statistics images explain the source's evaluation convention: its reported
losses always consume batch-statistics fake images even when displayed images use saved BN.
)
\toc

> Follow the three latent chunks and two conditional paths

The reference is [Brock, Donahue and Simonyan, Large Scale GAN Training for High Fidelity
Natural Image Synthesis (2018/2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.11096). This repository
retains a deliberately small 8x 8 teaching variant, not the paper's ImageNet architecture
or benchmark. G has one residual nearest-up block, four feature channels and a shared
raw class embedding. D has one residual down block and a spectral projection table.
The same true digit label conditions real and generated rows during fitting. No auxiliary
class head or requested-class accuracy metric is introduced.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/big_gan_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
