\alttext(Source, projection and total raw-score points for two discrete class conditions on the same features.)

> Check the modified orthogonal penalty on raw kernels

For a kernel M, the source adds lambda times the squared off-diagonal entries of
MᵀM. It excludes the Gram diagonal and applies the penalty to raw /W kernels,
not their spectrally normalized versions. A 2x 2 all-ones matrix with hand coefficient
lambda=.5 has off-diagonal Gram entries 2, giving penalty 4 and weight derivatives 4.
Other leaves contribute 0*sum(value), preserving defined zero partials. The raw G class
embedding, conditional biases and output BN affine vectors receive no orthogonal force.

The curve instead uses the actual source coefficient double(.5e-4f) on two unit
columns with correlation rho. Its penalty is 2*lambda*rho², displayed in units of 1e-4.
This is a soft off-diagonal penalty, not a unit-norm constraint or a guarantee of
orthogonality. The small generator's 4-to 64 stem is also overcomplete; no architectural
or convergence claim follows from reducing this scalar.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/big_gan_orthogonal.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n         = dm::nn;
  auto              t = n::DType::Float64;
  n::ParameterStore p(1, t);
  p.get("toy/W", {2, 2}, n::init::ones());
  p.get("bias", {2}, n::init::zeros());
  auto penalty  = n::models::ModifiedOrthogonalPenalty(p, .5);
  auto gradient = n::grad(penalty, p.parameters());
  if (penalty.item() != 4 || gradient[0].at({0, 0}) != 4 || n::sum(n::abs(gradient[1])).item() != 0)
    throw std::runtime_error("orthogonal toy");
  std::print(
      "All-ones kernel, lambda=.5: penalty={}, each kernel partial4; bias partial0.\n",
      penalty.item());
  std::vector<double> rho, value;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 20; ++i) {
    double r = i / 20.;
    p.set("toy/W", n::tensor({1., r, 0., std::sqrt(1 - r * r)}, {2, 2}, t));
    rho.push_back(r);
    value.push_back(1e4 * n::models::ModifiedOrthogonalPenalty(p).item());
  }
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.y_limits(0., 1.5)
      .lines(rho, value, "Source coefficient", {37, 99, 235}, 2.)
      .points(rho, value, "", {37, 99, 235}, 3.)
      .x_label("Correlation between two supplied unit columns")
      .y_label("Raw-kernel penalty, in units of1e-4")
      .title("Only off-diagonal Gram entries are penalized")
      .save_svg("build/big_gan_orthogonal.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
All-ones kernel, lambda=.5: penalty=4, each kernel partial4; bias partial0.
\image(figures/big_gan_orthogonal.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The hand coefficient.5 explains the derivative; the plotted curve uses the much smaller Float32-rounded source coefficient.5e-4.)
\alttext(Twenty-one raw-kernel penalties rise quadratically with column correlation, with no diagonal norm penalty.)

> Reject Gaussian proposals rather than clipping coordinates

`GaussianRejectionPrior` uses a local MT19937 stream, midpoint uniforms and Box-Muller
cosine then cached sine. A Double candidate is accepted when abs(candidate)<=limit,
then rounded once to the requested dtype. Every tested candidate, including rejects
and final acceptance, remains in the Float64 audit. An unused trailing sine is not
appended. Positive infinity gives ordinary Gaussian draws. Finite thresholds change
the accepted stream; they do not clamp or rescale coordinates. A strict proposal budget
below 1000 times the requested coordinate count bounds this teaching sampler's work.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/big_gan_rejection.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  auto                prior      = dm::nn::models::GaussianRejectionPrior(1, 12, 6907, .5);
  auto                candidates = prior.proposals.to_vector<double>();
  std::vector<double> ax, ay, rx, ry;
  auto                count = std::min<std::size_t>(24, candidates.size());
  double              low = -.5, high = .5;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
    double v = candidates[i];
    low      = std::min(low, v);
    high     = std::max(high, v);
    if (std::abs(v) <= .5) {
      ax.push_back(i + 1);
      ay.push_back(v);
    } else {
      rx.push_back(i + 1);
      ry.push_back(v);
    }
  }
  std::print(
      "Twelve accepted coordinates consume{} proposals; first{} shown, with rejection before final "
      "Float32 casting.\n",
      candidates.size(),
      count);
  double span = high - low;
  auto   p    = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.y_limits(low - .1 * span, high + .5 * span)
      .points(ax, ay, "Accepted Double candidate", {37, 99, 235}, 4.)
      .points(rx, ry, "Rejected Double candidate", {220, 38, 38}, 4.)
      .lines({1., double(count)}, {.5, .5}, "Absolute limit.5", {120, 120, 120}, 1.)
      .lines({1., double(count)}, {-.5, -.5}, "", {120, 120, 120}, 1.)
      .x_label("Tested proposal index; cosine then cached sine")
      .y_label("Unclipped Gaussian proposal")
      .title("Rejected values stay in the audit, not in the accepted bank")
      .save_svg("build/big_gan_rejection.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Twelve accepted coordinates consume26 proposals; first24 shown, with rejection before final Float32 casting.
\image(figures/big_gan_rejection.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Only the first 24 tested candidates are displayed; the saved prior retains the complete tested stream. This toy threshold is.5.)
\alttext(Blue accepted and red rejected Gaussian proposals, with horizontal positive/negative threshold references.)

> Native preparation and the resized digit pools

The program prepares `mnist-assets`, loads the checked saliency pools and resizes 28x 28
Float32 pixels to 8x 8 using half-pixel bilinear arithmetic in Double before a single
Float32 cast. Native resize reproduces all 89600 frozen resized pixels bit for bit.
Fitting/validation use disjoint original-training IDs; test IDs remain in the original
test range. Each class contributes 100 fitting and 20 validation/test rows. The preview
shows the first two fitting rows per class, without sample or model selection.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/big_gan_pools.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  auto home   = std::filesystem::path(dm::datasets::data_home());
  dm::datasets::prepare_example_data("mnist-assets", home);
  auto                      pools  = n::models::LoadSaliency(home / "saliency");
  auto                      small  = n::models::BigGanResize8(pools[0].images);
  auto                      labels = pools[0].labels.to_vector<std::int32_t>();
  std::vector<std::int64_t> rows;
  for (int c = 0; c < 10; ++c) {
    int seen = 0;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < 1000 && seen < 2; ++i)
      if (labels[i] == c) {
        rows.push_back(i);
        ++seen;
      }
  }
  n::save_image_grid(
      n::take_rows(small, rows), "build/big_gan_pools.png", 2, 0, 1, 4, 220 / 255., 8);
  std::print(
      "| pool | rows | per class | role |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| fitting |1000|100| updates |\n| "
      "validation |200|20| log only |\n| test |200|20| fixed final diagnostics |\n");
}
```
