> Inspect the two contributions to the first G gradient

The original experiment checks G partials as adversarial plus modified orthogonal.
The report also saves the first full captured gradient before any D/cache update for
this batch. The plot computes L 2 norms over all 789 G parameters and shows log 10(norm)
so the small orthogonal contribution remains visible. Non-kernel orthogonal partials
are defined zeros. The full sum agrees within the original 3e-5 Float32 tolerance;
matching component norms alone would not prove the vector equality, so every element
is checked too. These are initial-batch derivatives, not an optimizer or final-quality
ablation. The coefficient and schedule remain fixed throughout the run.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/big_gan_gradients.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::map<std::string, n::Tensor> a, o, f;
  for (const auto& [k, v] : n::load_npz("build/big_gan/first_adversarial_gradients.npz"))
    a.emplace(k, v);
  for (const auto& [k, v] : n::load_npz("build/big_gan/first_orthogonal_gradients.npz"))
    o.emplace(k, v);
  for (const auto& [k, v] : n::load_npz("build/big_gan/first_full_gradients.npz"))
    f.emplace(k, v);
  double       sa = 0, so = 0, sf = 0, error = 0;
  std::int64_t count = 0;
  for (const auto& [k, v] : a) {
    auto av = v.to_vector<double>(), ov = o.at(k).to_vector<double>(),
         fv = f.at(k).to_vector<double>();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < av.size(); ++i) {
      sa += av[i] * av[i];
      so += ov[i] * ov[i];
      sf += fv[i] * fv[i];
      error = std::max(error, std::abs(av[i] + ov[i] - fv[i]));
      ++count;
    }
  }
  if (count != 789 || error > 3e-5)
    throw std::runtime_error("G component vector sum");
  std::vector<double> norms{std::sqrt(sa), std::sqrt(so), std::sqrt(sf)}, logs;
  for (double value : norms)
    logs.push_back(std::log10(value));
  std::print(
      "All{} parameter partials checked; max sum residual={}; L2 norms: adversarial={}, "
      "orthogonal={}, full={}.\n",
      count,
      error,
      norms[0],
      norms[1],
      norms[2]);
  double low  = *std::min_element(logs.begin(), logs.end()),
         high = *std::max_element(logs.begin(), logs.end());
  auto p      = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.y_limits(low - .5, high + 2.)
      .points({1.}, {logs[0]}, "Adversarial", {37, 99, 235}, 5.)
      .points({2.}, {logs[1]}, "Modified orthogonal", {22, 163, 74}, 5.)
      .points({3.}, {logs[2]}, "Full G gradient", {220, 38, 38}, 5.)
      .x_label("Component: adversarial1; orthogonal2; full3")
      .y_label("log10 L2 parameter-gradient norm")
      .title("Check the full vector sum as well as its scale")
      .save_svg("build/big_gan_gradients.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
All789 parameter partials checked; max sum residual=6.9548962144949655e-09; L2 norms: adversarial=10.100318581464231, orthogonal=1.7345970843958127e-06, full=10.100318578130377.
\image(figures/big_gan_gradients.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The full archive also keeps 24 captured BN values; only 789 parameter derivatives enter the norm. Every component vector coordinate is checked separately from the plot.)
\alttext(Logarithmic parameter-gradient norms for initial adversarial, orthogonal and full G contributions.)

> Verification and limits

The full promoted-source run and the editable native experiment preserve all 23 original
archives/319761 values exactly in dtype, shape and value bytes, both TSVs, all three source
PNGs and non-timing metrics. Ten phase probes additionally match 15268 native values
across initialization, spectral advances, selected gradients, Adam and captured BN writes.
Direct combined initialization matches all 1372 checkpoint values; six native prior banks
match 34069 accepted/audit values, and native resize matches 89600 pixel values exactly.

NumPy independently imports no datamunge and performs no fitting. Its Double calculations
check 84708 forward values,4740 native derivative/capture values,74 final scalar finite
differences,89600 resized pixels,19669 exact rejection proposals and 14400 accepted prior
coordinates. It checks class/projection, hierarchy and truncation controls, all 3000 fitting
row visits, phase ownership and bit-exact first moving-statistic updates. Float32 native
normalization derivatives are compared with measured numerical tolerances; they are not
claimed bit identical to independent Double arithmetic. The native-to-native comparisons
above are strict. The extra full-gradient and projection-decomposition archives receive
separate independent checks.

Native, Python and R component checks cover owning nested records, malformed inputs,
source-rounded captures, stopped state paths, higher derivatives, all hierarchical chunks,
projection dependence and explicit writes. They do not complete independent host-language
full experiment loops. This is one short fixed run, without a seed sweep, uncertainty study,
requested-class recognition metric or paper-scale benchmark. Conditioning responses and
rejection controls demonstrate mechanisms; no convergence, semantic disentanglement,
certified Lipschitz bound or realistic-generation result follows from those checks alone.
